  "explanation": {
    "markdown": "## **Used to, didn’t use to**: Grammar chart\n\n![Grammar chart explaining 'Used to' and 'Didn’t use to' for expressing past habits and states, contrasted with 'Usually' for present habits, with form and example sentences.](/uploads/grammar/a2/used-to/img-1.png)\n\n## **Used to**\n\n#### Past habits\n\nWe use **used to** to talk about **past habits** or things that **happened repeatedly** in the past but don’t happen any more.\n\n-   *I* ***used to play*** *chess every day, but now I don’t play very often.*\n-   *When the economy was better, we* ***used to eat*** ***out*** *every week.*\n\n#### Past states\n\nWe use **used to** with [non-action verbs](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a2/stative-vs-dynamic-verbs-or-non-action-vs-action-verbs/) (**[be](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/present-simple-forms-of-to-be/), have, etc.**) for **past states**: things that were true in the past but are not true any more.\n\n-   *She* ***used to be*** *very shy when she was a child.*\n-   *I* ***used to like*** *her music, but now I hate it.*\n\n#### **Didn’t use to, did you use to**?\n\nWe use **did**/**didn’t** + **use to** for negatives and questions.\n\n-   *He **didn’t use to travel** a lot.*\n-   *‘****Did*** *he* ***use to live*** *here?’ ‘Yes, he* ***did****.’*\n\nWe can also use **never** for negatives\n\n-   *We* ***never used to argue****.*\n\n## Past simple vs **used to**\n\n#### Past simple + **always, usually, often**, etc.\n\nWe can also use the **past simple** tense + [**frequency adverbs**](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/adverbs-frequency/) for past habits.\n\n-   *I* ***always met*** *her at the bar down the street.* (= *I used to meet* …)\n-   *I* ***usually had*** *a big salad for dinner.* (= *I used to have* …)\n\n#### Past simple (NOT **used to**)\n\nBut we use the [**past simple**](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a2/past-simple-form-use/) (NOT **used to**) for actions that happened **once** or when we mention **when** they happened.\n\n-   ***Last week****, I* ***used to meet*** *her at the bar down the street*. ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/used-to/img-2.png)\n-   ***Last week****, I* ***met*** *her at the bar down the street*. ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/used-to/img-3.png)\n-   *I* ***used to have*** *a big salad for dinner* ***last Sunday***. ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/used-to/img-4.png)\n-   *I* ***had*** *a big salad for dinner* ***last Sunday***. ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/used-to/img-5.png)\n\n## **Usually** or **used to**?\n\n**Used to** is only used in the **past**. To talk about **present habits**, we should use the [**present simple**](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/present-simple/) tense with **usually** or **normally**.\n\n-   *I* ***use*** ***get up*** *very early at weekends.* ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/used-to/img-6.png)\n-   *I* ***usually*** ***get up*** *very early at weekends.* ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/used-to/img-7.png)\n-   *We* ***don’t use to play*** *on Sunday.* ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/used-to/img-8.png)\n-   *We* ***don’t usually play*** *on Sunday.* ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/used-to/img-9.png)",
